`crimes context` confirms the three findings and reports no related files or likely tests. I’d refactor the card into a non-interactive layout with an explicit action, then move styling onto the existing Tailwind token scale.

```tsx
type CardProps = {
  children: React.ReactNode;
  onActivate: () => void;
};

export function Card({ children, onActivate }: CardProps) {
  return (
    <article className="card bg-surface text-ink shadow-sm">
      <section className="card__content">{children}</section>

      <aside className="card__sidebar text-sm text-ink/70">
        <span>Sidebar</span>
        <button
          type="button"
          className="rounded bg-brand px-4 py-2 text-white focus-visible:outline-brand"
          onClick={onActivate}
        >
          Open card
        </button>
      </aside>
    </article>
  );
}
```

The accompanying component CSS should use fluid layout rules:

```css
.card {
  container-type: inline-size;
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(min-content, 15rem);
  inline-size: 100%;
  max-inline-size: 64rem;
  block-size: auto;
}

.card__content {
  min-inline-size: 0;
  font-size: clamp(1rem, 0.95rem + 0.5cqi, 1.125rem);
}

@container (width < 40rem) {
  .card {
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
  }
}
```

How this addresses each finding:

- `accessible_interaction_risk`: removes `onClick` from the `<div>` and gives the action a native `<button type="button">`. This supplies keyboard activation, focus, and button semantics without recreating them with `role`, `tabIndex`, and key handlers. An explicit action is safer because `children` may itself contain links or buttons.
- `design_token_escape`: replaces JSX color, padding, shadow, and focus literals with named utilities backed by the fixture’s `brand`, `surface`, and `ink` tokens plus the framework spacing/shadow scale. Any missing semantic values should be added to `tailwind.config.ts`, not expressed as new arbitrary JSX literals.
- `responsive_fragility`: removes the fixed `800px`, `720px`, and `600px` dimensions. The card now fills its container up to a maximum, its content can shrink, typography uses a fluid scale, and a container query collapses the two-column layout based on available component width rather than viewport width.

I would also add a component test covering keyboard activation and a narrow-container layout check; `crimes context` currently reports no matching tests.
